【Butterfly】

Two Poems on the Qujiang River: The Second
Du Fu
Returning from court each day, I pawn my spring clothes;
Daily by the river's edge, I return drunk to the full.
Wine debts are common wherever I go;
To live to seventy is rare since ancient times.
Butterflies flit deep among the flowers, seen clearly;
Dragonflies touch the water lightly, flying leisurely.
I send word to the scenery to linger together;
For a while, let us enjoy each other, do not part.

Autumn Butterfly
Bai Juyi
Autumn flowers, a hazy purple;
Autumn butterflies, a downy yellow.
Flowers droop, butterflies are new and small;
Flying and playing, west and east among the thickets.
As dusk falls, the cool breeze comes;
Flowers fall in profusion upon the thickets.
Deep in the night, the white dew is cold;
The butterflies have already died within the thickets.
Born at dawn, dead by evening, all alike;
Each kind follows its own nature.
Have you not seen the thousand-year crane?
It often perches on the hundred-foot pine.

【Bee】

Drinking Alone
Du Fu
My steps take me deep into the woods at dusk;
I open the wine jar, late for drinking alone.
Looking up, a bee clings to falling catkins;
Marching ants climb a withered pear tree.
My shallow talent shames true hermits;
In secluded偏僻, I find my own joy.
I never had the mind for high office;
It's not that I scorn the present age.

Solitary Enjoyment in a Leisure Garden
Bai Juyi
Quiet in the suburban garden past noon;
With clearing weather, the scenery turns fresh.
Rain adds color to the mountain's air;
The wind borrows the water's spirit.
How to pass the long day?
Roaming alone, what is dear to me?
Divine birds befriend the gentleman;
Fragrant trees lean against the fair lady.
Ants battle, kings fight for meat;
Snails move, their homes follow their bodies.
Paired butterflies know they are a couple;
Divided bees show their ruler and subjects.
Though their wriggling forms are small,
Their carefree natures are all equal.
Who knows how many specks of dust
Lie between the Roc and the quail?

